item.content:encoded:
Anyone who's read “bro-coder” C from before that was a name will recognize
this idiom:    var_t1_2 = 0x7F;
    ...
    do {
        ...
        var_t1_2 -= 1;
    } while (var_t1_2 >= 0);
Why does the loop count backwards? It's because comparison with zero
is cheaper on some (many) platforms. Some people would reverse the loop
manually, but compilers quickly managed to do that themselves. So you want:   for (i = 0; i < 0x80; ++i) {
      ...
   }
and tomorrow, we'll have a look at what's inside the loop as well.

Say you see this lovely chunk of decompiled code:    var_v1 = &sp10
    var_v0 = D_80010040;
    temp_s4 = var_v1;
    sp110 = &sp2C
    sp114 = &sp30
    do {
        var_v1->unk0 = (s32) var_v0->unk0;
        var_v1->unk4 = (s32) var_v0[1];
        var_v1->unk8 = (s32) var_v0[2];
        var_v1->unkC = (s32) var_v0[3];
        var_v0 += 0x10;
        var_v1 += 0x10;
    } while (var_v0 != &D_8001040[0x3C]);
    var_v1->unk0 = (s32) var_v0->unk0;
    var_v1->unk4 = (s32) var_v0[1];
    var_v1->unk8 = (s32) var_v0[2];
If you ignore the fact that m2c wrote  for  (it has a bit of an
annoying habit to do that), this is obviously something copying 16 bytes
at a time and then 12 more bytes at the end. But would a programmer ever do this?
Nope, this is an inlined, unrolled memcpy.This is known in GCC as , and it can happen either implicitly,
or by some sort of lowering from a struct or array copy (e.g., through initialization
of a stack array). Depending a bit on whether the variable is known to be aligned
or not, you'll either get just  or some evil / sequence; I'm always
struggling a bit to change one to the other. In any case, what you want here is:    __builtin_memcpy(&sp10, D_80010040, 0x4C);
and then make  the right type instead of a scalar, ideally so that you can
use  on it. Or, in some cases, a direct initialization:    s32 foo[] = { 1, 2, 3, ...more data from D_80010040... };

Here's one that m2c usually gets right itself, but sometimes cannot:    if (var_v1_7 != 1) {
        if (var_v1_7 < 2) { 
            if (var_v1_7 != 0) {
                spF0 = 40; 
            } else {
                vram_x = cmt46->vram_x;
                vram_y = cmt46->vram_y;
                spF0 = 40;
            }
        } else if (var_v1_7 != 2) {
            if (var_v1_7 != 3) {
Repeated comparisons against the same variable, especially in a nested form
that could be binary search, should remind you of one thing: A switch.
Usually, the compiler implements a switch with a jump table, but not always;
if there are few conditions, or the range is too large for a reasonably-sized
table, it will do some ifs instead (or, enough ifs to get within the range
of a table). So what you want here is more like:    switch (var_v1_7) {
    case 0:
       vram_x = cmt46->vram_x;
       vram_y = cmt46->vram_y;
       spF0 = 40;
       break;
    case 1:
       spF0 = 40;
       break;
    case 2:
       ...
    }
Remember that sometimes, you may need to insert cases that are not explicitly
mentioned in the ifs (and/or a default case) to get everything to match back
properly.

The v0 register on MIPS is special in that it is the return register
(like rax is on x86-64), so when you see code like this:if (x == 3) {
    var_v0 = 1;
} else if (y == 4) {
    ...
    var_v0 = 3;
}
var_v0 = 5;
return var_v0;
then you have to consider the possibility that the intended match is:if (x == 3) {
    return 1;
} else if (y == 4) {
    ...
    return 3;
} else {
    return 5;
}
Whether you put the last return in an else block or not is irrelevant
for the match, of course, it's purely a matter of taste.

This is more of a note to myself since I keep forgetting and "rediscovering" this technique every time I need it, but if it helps you, great!The JavaScript way is to toggle displaying the menu based on a button click. For instance, here's an example in Svelte:[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]<script lang="ts">
	let showMenu = $state(false);
</script>

<button onclick={() => (showMenu = !showMenu)}>
	{#if showMenu}
		Hide menu
	{:else}
		Show menu
	{/if}
</button>
<nav class:hidden={!showMenu}>
	<ul>
		<li>Home</li>
		<li>Blog</li>
		<li>Foo</li>
		<li>Bar</li>
	</ul>
</nav>

<style>
	.hidden {
		display: none;
	}
</style>But there is a hack to use a hidden checkbox and some CSS to achieve the same thing without any JavaScript. The example here turns the menu button into a label that toggles the checkbox, and uses the checkbox state to determine when to show the menu via the next-sibling combinator.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]<input type="checkbox" id="menu-state" />
<div class="menu-ctn">
	<label class="menu-toggle" for="menu-state">
		<span class="menu-closed">Show menu</span>
		<span class="menu-open">Hide menu</span>
	</label>
	<nav class="menu-open">
		<ul>
			<li>Home</li>
			<li>Blog</li>
			<li>Foo</li>
			<li>Bar</li>
		</ul>
	</nav>
</div>

<style>
	#menu-state {
		display: none;
	}
	.menu-open {
		display: none;
	}
	.menu-closed {
		display: block;
	}
	#menu-state:checked + .menu-ctn .menu-open {
		display: block;
	}
	#menu-state:checked + .menu-ctn .menu-closed {
		display: none;
	}
</style>

Today, for something completely different:s.x = (s16) ((s32) (temp_v0 << 0x10) >> 0x11);
MIPS has 16-bit loads and stores, but not 16-bit (signed) arithmetic,
so if it wants to do that, it needs to sign-extend to 32-bit first.
And it does that by first shifting up by 16 so that the 16-bit sign bit is
at the 32-bit sign bit, and then it can do an arithmetic shift afterwards.
So what you want is:s16 temp_v0;

s.x = temp_v0 >> 1;
Sometimes, it can be pretty stubborn, and you'll need to go through
memory or similar before GCC really inserts the shift patterns you want.

Of course, you could just use the modern choice. Here, I've shortened what Schade uses into one line of CSS (because I can). As noted, this is a fairly new feature so not every browser supports this.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.].underline-me {
	text-decoration: underline red solid 3px;
}Since some browsers don't support , here are the alternatives I could think of.The workaround Schade describes is using , though it has a gap between the text in the line.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am <span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span> within the sentence..underline-me {
	border-bottom: red solid 3px;
}Probably the better way is to create an  element, which gets very close, but displays above the text (covering the letters 'g' and 'y').[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am <span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span> within the sentence..underline-me {
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me::after {
	content: '';
	position: absolute;
	width: 100%;
	height: 3px;
	background-color: red;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 2px;
}If you use a container (in this case, two nested s instead of just one), then you can get very close with the underline displaying below the text.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am
<span class="underline-me-ctn">
	<span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span>
</span>
within the sentence..underline-me-ctn {
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me {
	z-index: 10;
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me-ctn::after {
	content: '';
	position: absolute;
	width: 100%;
	height: 3px;
	background-color: red;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 2px;
}::after in a container with text strokeAnd now if you add a stroke to the text via , you're pretty much there, though this may not be feasible in some situations (e.g. gradient or image background behind the text). Support for  is not that much better than  though, so make of that what you will.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am
<span class="underline-me-ctn">
	<span class="underline-me-outline">underlining this yap</span>
	<span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span>
</span>
within the sentence..underline-me-ctn {
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me-outline {
	z-index: 5;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	color: var(--background);
	-webkit-text-stroke: 0.3em var(--background);
}
.underline-me {
	z-index: 10;
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me-ctn::after {
	content: '';
	position: absolute;
	width: 100%;
	height: 3px;
	background-color: red;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 2px;
}
